---
/**
 * Public terms of service. Two audiences, deliberately separated:
 *   - people using the services we run (this site, the hosted instance);
 *   - people using the software, whose relationship is with the AGPL-3.0
 *     licence in the repository, not with this page.
 *
 * Nothing here may narrow the licence: if these terms and the AGPL ever
 * disagree about the software, the AGPL wins. Section 10 says so explicitly.
 */
import Legal from "../layouts/Legal.astro";
import { appUrl, legalEmail, operator, operatorSite, repo } from "../site";

const license = `${repo}/blob/main/LICENSE`;
---

<Legal
  heading="Terms of Service"
  description="The terms for using the sharp website and the hosted instance we run — acceptable use, no-warranty, liability, and how they relate to the AGPL-3.0 licence."
  path="/terms"
>
  <div class="legal-note">
    <p>
      <strong>Read this first — scope.</strong> These terms govern the two services we
      operate: this website (<code>sharp.davideghiotto.it</code>) and the hosted instance at
      <code>app.sharp.davideghiotto.it</code>. They do <strong>not</strong> govern the sharp
      software itself — that is licensed to you under
      <a href={license} rel="noopener">AGPL-3.0</a>, and nothing on this page adds a condition
      to it (<a href="#software">section 10</a>). If you use sharp on someone else's server,
      your terms are with whoever runs it.
    </p>
  </div>

  <div class="legal-toc">
    <strong>On this page</strong>
    <ol>
      <li><a href="#agreement">The agreement</a></li>
      <li><a href="#accounts">Accounts</a></li>
      <li><a href="#use">Acceptable use</a></li>
      <li><a href="#content">Your content</a></li>
      <li><a href="#service">The hosted instance: what you can expect</a></li>
      <li><a href="#ai">The AI assistant</a></li>
      <li><a href="#calls">Calls and guest links</a></li>
      <li><a href="#thirdparty">Third-party integrations</a></li>
      <li><a href="#termination">Suspension and termination</a></li>
      <li><a href="#software">The software and the licence</a></li>
      <li><a href="#warranty">No warranty</a></li>
      <li><a href="#liability">Limitation of liability</a></li>
      <li><a href="#law">Governing law and disputes</a></li>
      <li><a href="#changes">Changes to these terms</a></li>
    </ol>
  </div>

  <h2 id="agreement">1. The agreement</h2>
  <p>
    These terms are a contract between you and <strong>{operator}</strong>, an individual
    based in Italy operating the sharp project ("we", "us"). By visiting this website or using
    the hosted instance you accept them. If you do not, do not use the services — the software
    is still yours to run under its licence.
  </p>
  <p>
    You must be at least 16 years old to hold an account. If you are using the services for an
    organisation, you confirm you are allowed to accept these terms on its behalf.
  </p>

  <h2 id="accounts">2. Accounts</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>Give a real, working email address; we need it for password resets and for service
      notices.</li>
    <li>You are responsible for what happens under your account, so keep your password or
      passkey to yourself and do not share credentials. Tell us at
      <a href={`mailto:${legalEmail}`}>{legalEmail}</a> if you think it has been compromised.</li>
    <li>One person, one account. Do not impersonate anyone, and do not register on behalf of
      someone who has been removed.</li>
    <li>The hosted instance is a single shared workspace. Anything you post in a public channel
      is visible to every member of that workspace — treat it accordingly.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2 id="use">3. Acceptable use</h2>
  <p>Do not use the services to:</p>
  <ul>
    <li>break the law, or post content that is illegal where you or we are — in particular
      child sexual abuse material, terrorist content, or non-consensual intimate images, all of
      which we report to the competent authorities;</li>
    <li>harass, threaten, defame or stalk anyone, or post hateful content targeting people for
      who they are;</li>
    <li>infringe someone's copyright, trademark, trade secret or privacy;</li>
    <li>distribute malware, phishing pages, or content designed to deceive;</li>
    <li>send unsolicited bulk messages, or use the workspace for advertising;</li>
    <li>attack the service — probing, brute-forcing, denial of service, exploiting a
      vulnerability instead of reporting it, or bypassing rate limits and quotas;</li>
    <li>circumvent the authorisation model to read channels, documents or tasks you were not
      given access to, including by prompting the AI assistant to do it for you;</li>
    <li>scrape the instance, or resell access to it;</li>
    <li>use it as generic file storage, a media-distribution host, or a crypto-mining or
      compute-relay endpoint;</li>
    <li>consume shared resources — storage, call capacity, AI credits — in a way that degrades
      the service for everyone else.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    Security research is welcome and we would rather hear from you than not: report findings
    privately to <a href={`mailto:${legalEmail}`}>{legalEmail}</a> and give us reasonable time
    to fix them. Do not test against other people's accounts or data.
  </p>

  <h2 id="content">4. Your content</h2>
  <p>
    <strong>You keep every right you have in what you post.</strong> We claim no ownership of
    your messages, files, documents, canvases or tasks. You grant us only the narrow,
    non-exclusive, royalty-free licence needed to actually run the service — to store,
    transmit, index, back up and display your content to the people you shared it with, and to
    generate the derived forms the features need (thumbnails, search indexes, embeddings for
    the assistant). That licence ends when the content is deleted, subject to the backup
    window in the <a href="/privacy#retention">privacy policy</a>.
  </p>
  <p>
    You are responsible for having the rights to what you upload. We do not review content
    proactively, but we may remove anything that breaks section 3 or the law once we know
    about it. Report content to <a href={`mailto:${legalEmail}`}>{legalEmail}</a>.
  </p>

  <h2 id="service">5. The hosted instance: what you can expect</h2>
  <p>
    Be clear-eyed about this one. <code>app.sharp.davideghiotto.it</code> is offered
    <strong>free of charge, best-effort, and with no service level agreement</strong>. It is
    run by one person, largely as a live deployment of an open-source project. Concretely:
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li>there is <strong>no uptime guarantee</strong>, no support commitment and no response
      time;</li>
    <li>features may change, be reduced or be removed, and the instance may be taken down for
      maintenance without notice;</li>
    <li>quotas — storage, upload size, call participants and duration, AI usage — may be
      introduced or lowered at any time;</li>
    <li>we may <strong>wipe or reset</strong> the instance, or shut it down entirely. Where it
      is practicable we will give at least 30 days' notice in the workspace so you can export
      your data;</li>
    <li>keep your own copy of anything you cannot afford to lose. The reliable way to depend on
      sharp is to <a href={repo} rel="noopener">run it yourself</a>.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    None of the above limits your statutory rights as a consumer under Italian or EU law where
    those apply.
  </p>

  <h2 id="ai">6. The AI assistant</h2>
  <p>
    If enabled, the assistant answers from your workspace content using a third-party language
    model. Its answers are <strong>generated, not verified</strong>: they can be wrong,
    incomplete or out of date, and they are not professional advice of any kind. Check anything
    that matters before relying on it. Your question and the excerpts retrieved to answer it
    are sent to the model provider — see the
    <a href="/privacy#processors">privacy policy</a>. Do not use the assistant to try to reach
    content you are not authorised to see; retrieval enforces the same permissions as the rest
    of the app, and attempting to defeat that breaches section 3.
  </p>

  <h2 id="calls">7. Calls and guest links</h2>
  <p>
    Calls are relayed live and not recorded by us. Transcription and meeting notes exist only
    when a participant switches them on. <strong>Recording or transcribing other people can
    require their consent</strong> — in Italy and much of the EU it does — and getting that
    consent is your responsibility, not ours. Tell the room before you start.
  </p>
  <p>
    A call link admits whoever holds it, including people without an account, under a
    room-bound guest session. Treat a link as a key: share it only with people you mean to
    invite, and do not post it publicly.
  </p>

  <h2 id="thirdparty">8. Third-party integrations</h2>
  <p>
    Connecting Google Calendar, GitHub, a GIF provider or anything else is your choice, and
    your use of those services stays governed by their own terms and privacy policies. We are
    not responsible for what they do, for outages on their side, or for data you ask us to send
    them. Disconnect an integration and the flow stops.
  </p>

  <h2 id="termination">9. Suspension and termination</h2>
  <p>
    You can stop at any time and ask us to delete your account (<a href="/privacy#rights">how</a>).
    We may suspend or terminate an account that breaches these terms, that creates legal risk
    for us or other users, or that is being used to attack the service. Where the breach is not
    serious we will warn you first and give you a chance to fix it; where it is serious —
    illegal content, an active attack — we may act immediately. On termination your right to
    use the services ends and your data is deleted according to the retention schedule in the
    privacy policy. Sections 4, 11, 12 and 13 survive.
  </p>

  <h2 id="software">10. The software and the licence</h2>
  <p>
    sharp the software is licensed under the <a href={license} rel="noopener">GNU Affero
    General Public License, version 3</a>. Run it, study it, modify it, redistribute it, host
    it commercially — the licence tells you what you may do and the one condition attached
    (publish your modifications under the same licence if you offer it over a network). There
    is no separate commercial edition, no contributor licence agreement, and no paid tier.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>These terms do not restrict the licence, and if they ever appear to conflict with
    it in respect of the software, the licence prevails.</strong> Downloads and source access
    happen on GitHub under GitHub's terms.
  </p>
  <p>
    Two things the licence does not give you: the project's name and marks, and our
    infrastructure. Do not present a modified build as official sharp, or imply we endorse or
    support your deployment. Content on this website — copy, images, the design — is
    © {operator} and is not covered by the software licence.
  </p>

  <h2 id="warranty">11. No warranty</h2>
  <p>
    The website and the hosted instance are provided <strong>"as is" and "as available"</strong>,
    with no warranty of any kind, express or implied, including fitness for a particular
    purpose, merchantability, non-infringement, uninterrupted or error-free operation, or that
    data will not be lost. The software carries the separate disclaimer stated in the AGPL. If
    you are a consumer, mandatory warranties under applicable law are unaffected by this
    paragraph.
  </p>

